.TH "grommunio\-admin\-passwd" "1" "" "" ""
.SH Name
grommunio\-admin passwd \[em] Set user password
.SH Synopsis
\f[B]grommunio\-admin passwd\f[R] [\f[I]\-a\f[R]] [\f[I]\-l LENGTH\f[R]]
[\f[I]\-p PASSWORD\f[R]] [\f[I]\-\-password\-stdin\f[R]] [\f[I]USER\f[R]]
.SH Description
.PP
Set user password.
.PD 0
.P
.PD
If no user is specified, the password is set for the \f[I]admin\f[R]
user, which is created automatically if necessary.
.PD 0
.P
.PD
If none of \f[I]\-a\f[R], \f[I]\-p\f[R] or
\f[I]\-\-password\-stdin\f[R] is provided, the user is prompted for a
password.
.SH Options
.TP
\f[CR]USER\f[R]
User to set password for (default \f[I]admin\f[R])
.TP
\f[CR]\-a\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-auto\f[R]
Automatically generate a password
.TP
\f[CR]\-l LENGTH\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-length LENGTH\f[R]
Length of the automatically generated password (default 16)
.TP
\f[CR]\-p PASSWORD\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-password PASSWORD\f[R]
Password to set (do not prompt).
Note that the password is visible to every local user in the process
list for as long as the command runs; on multi\-user hosts use
\f[I]\-\-password\-stdin\f[R] instead.
.TP
\f[CR]\-\-password\-stdin\f[R]
Read the password from the first line of standard input (do not
prompt).
The trailing newline is stripped; any further input is ignored.
.SH See Also
\f[B]grommunio\-admin\f[R](1), \f[B]grommunio\-admin\-user\f[R](1)
